"Surah al-Mutaffifin is the last surah revealed in Mecca, and according to some narrations, the first surah revealed in Medina. Woe to those who defraud in measure and weight! When they take measure from people, they take it in full, but when they measure or weigh for others, they give short. These verses sternly warn those who make a habit of cheating in measure and weight.\n\nThe word \"Wayl\" means severe punishment, a valley in Hell, and similar meanings. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said: \"Wayl is a valley in Hell down which a disbeliever falls for forty years before reaching its bottom.\" This warning shows that even one who cheats ever so slightly in measure and weight deserves a severe punishment. Indeed, a Bedouin said to Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan: \"If a defrauder (mutaffif) is threatened like this for stealing a tiny amount, then what will your state be when you take the property of Muslims without measure?\"\n\nCheating in measure and weight shakes the foundation of right and justice. The heavens and the earth stand upon a measure and balance. Two things are required for accurate measurement: the measuring instrument being complete, and the conscience of the measurer being upright. People cannot be free from fraud unless they view the rights of others as their own rights. This is why Allah commands the rectification of measure and weight first.\n\nDo those who commit this fraud not think that they will be resurrected for a tremendous Day? On that Day, people will stand before the Lord of the worlds, and everyone will receive their due right. It is this belief that prevents fraud. The record of the wicked is in Sijjin. Sijjin is a sealed book; the deeds of the wicked are recorded there. Woe that Day to the deniers! They deny the Day of Recompense and call Our verses \"tales of the ancients.\"\n\nTheir hearts have rusted with the sins they have earned. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said: \"When a servant commits a sin, a black spot appears on his heart. If he repents, it is erased; if he repeats it, the spot grows until it covers his heart. That is what Allah refers to as 'ran'.\" For this reason, they will be deprived of seeing their Lord in the Hereafter and will enter Hell.\n\nThe word Sijjin means \"constricting, confining\" or \"dungeon.\" The record of the wicked is recorded in a book beneath the seventh earth, where Iblis is located. This book is sealed and accepts no alteration whatsoever. The record of the righteous, on the other hand, is in Illiyyun, which signifies elevation. Sijjin, however, symbolizes abasement and humiliation.\n\nIn verse 17, it is stated that on that Day it will be said to the deniers: 'This is what you used to deny.' The word 'Kalla' (Nay) at the beginning of verse 18 warns against defrauding in measure and weight, encourages goodness, and reminds that one must turn away from the path of the wicked. For the record of the righteous is surely in Illiyyun.\n\nThe word Illiyyun has been explained differently by lexicographers and commentators. Abu al-Fath ibn Jinni states that it is the plural of the word 'illiyy', while al-Zajjaj says that its grammatical inflection (i'rab) is like a plural. In tafsirs, meanings such as the seventh heaven, the right leg of the Throne, Sidrat al-Muntaha, and 'infinitely higher and higher' have been given. According to some, Illiyyun is the place where the angels' registers of deeds are kept and, as the opposite of Sijjin, the register in which good deeds are inscribed. In verses 19-21, it is stated that Illiyyun is a sealed book, witnessed by the angels brought near to Allah.\n\nIn verses 22-28, it is described that the righteous are in bliss in Paradise. They are served a pure drink, rahiq. Rahiq is a wine that has no mixture, is clear, delicious, and does not cause a headache. Its vessel is sealed with musk. The mixture of this wine is from Tasnim; Tasnim is the highest of the springs of Paradise. While the servants brought near to Allah (al-muqarrabun) drink it pure, the other righteous servants (al-abrar) drink it mixed. This is also connected to the command to compete in the verse.\n\nVerses 29-33 describe how the criminals used to mock the believers in the world. The polytheists of Mecca would laugh at the poor believers, wink at one another when they passed by them, and amuse themselves when they returned to their homes. They would accuse them of being astray. Yet, they were not sent as keepers over the believers; they had no right to these judgments.\n\nIn verses 34-36, it is stated that on the Day of Resurrection, the believers will laugh at the disbelievers. Seated on couches in Paradise, the believers watch the disbelievers enter Hell. The question asked, 'Have the disbelievers been rewarded for what they used to do?', is the retribution for their mocking in the world. This is also to mock them in return. In conclusion, everyone receives the recompense of their deeds."